dead cells is a game that just recently  came out of early access and what do you  know I was given a copy of the game  first time for everything I've had a lot  of fun playing it so far so I thought  I'd give my two cents on it bear in mind  that this is coming from someone who  didn't have to actually pay for the game  firstly it is in no way shape or form a  Souls like it advertises quote souls  light combat not souls like souls light  and action in the sense that it is quote  tough but fair but I think this is a  pretty big stretch which I don't really  get the game is great on its own so the  devs don't need to try and string a  Souls comparison when it really isn't  needed I would never consider dead cells  combat to be souls like or souls light  or any other souls like like word but  I'll get more into that once we actually  understand what the game is so let's  explain what that cells is as the steam  page says it's a rogue light  Metroidvania there's that word light  again okay maybe the death is just like  the word light a lot the core elements  that define this game are the features  that would qualify it to be a roguelike  and those that would qualify it to be a  Metroidvania the devs have also opted to  coin and use rogue dania as a descriptor  get it like rogue light and Metroidvania  which I guess is apt but makes me wonder  how many game names in genres we can add  into one word until we arrive at our own  video game ship of theseus in the past  year or so I've gotten to play some  great games there would actually be  really relevant in comparing to dead  cells hollow night for the metroidvania  side and Binding of Isaac and enter the  gungeon for the rogue side I really  enjoyed all of those games and think  that there are some great modern takes  on their respective genres I wouldn't be  afraid to speak of dead cells in a  similar breath as those games so if  you've played them that should give you  a picture of my attitude Island dead  cells going into this video so let's  break each side of this rogue Vania down  in suit a Metroidvania x' each level in  dead cells is like a labyrinth you  explore and weave your way through as  you go you may discover power-ups or  dead ends or mini-bosses or anything in  between there is a constant need to  backtrack and sometimes areas and  enemies will be different on the second  go-around though the tediousness or  meticulous nest depending on how you  look at it of traditional Metroidvania  backtracking is not to present in dead  cells because of the plethora of  teleportation stations you probably come  across one every 10 seconds and each one  lets you teleport to any other one  you've revealed letting you move between  the entire explored map relatively  instantly so it's not sticking to the  metroidvania formula completely which  makes sense it's not a Metroidvania it's  a rogue vania now we're going to start  treading into the rogue territory a bit  in debt cells the gameplay is instant  out in runs as you progress through the  levels if you die you lose all your  required weapons power-ups and  currencies in this case the currencies  are gold and cells they're sent all the  way back to the start and have to  restart the whole run-through if you're  familiar with binding of issac or enter  the gungeon  then this concept of runs is just the  same and like those games in each run  you'll unlock and pick up items and  power-ups that change your attacks and  increase your damage or other stats you  can also unlock certain things that  remain permanently unlocked even across  deaths which alter gameplay however back  to the metroidvania land these permanent  unlocks are more significant than in  traditional rogue lights and that they  have much more broader consequences  there are things like giving you the  ability to permanently access a new zone  heal multiple times instead of once or  Never consistently find better gear and  drops and progress faster overall and  just to flesh out my description a bit  as you progress through the levels  you'll find new weapons gadgets and  grenades that you adopt a variety of  different playstyles especially when  paired with choosing which of your three  stats you want to level most of the  weapons and gadgets do damage directly  correlated with the level of your stats  red weapons do higher damage the more  the red skill is leveled and the same  goes with the other two colors each  color also has a general focus for  example red as a focus on higher damage  and green has a focus on higher  sustainability more HP and HP recovery  now that we have a working idea of what  constitutes this rogue dania known as  dead cells let's talk about gameplay and  how this game is the darksouls of games  that aren't like dark souls and  shouldn't be compared to dark souls  first off the similarities you have an  invincible role you have limited heals  that were filled chuck  points moving on to differences you have  no stamina bar the game is 2d you have  no light or heavy attacks there are no  proper weapon upgrade trees leveling is  based on item pickups not killing  enemies and the biggest one is that the  combat is not slow and deliberate it's  really fast and if I can save this  without any negative connotation its  spammy when I'm hitting scrubs I'm  mashing my x and y buttons as fast as I  can and I don't mean to say this is a  bad thing I really enjoy the fast pace  when I get into a good rhythm of slicing  enemies and zipping through the map the  game shines brilliantly even the heavier  slower weapons still give themselves to  a certain speed of progressing through  the level as you may only need to hit  enemies once or twice which means you  can keep up the overall pace this is at  odds with the souls mentality of slow  and deliberate as I mentioned before I  would describe running through the  worlds of lothric or Lordran as a fairly  meticulous experience and the combat of  souls games is such that you always have  to be careful because one hit could lead  to your demise dead cells is all about  keeping up pace the game even rewards  you generously for reaching upper levels  in a short amount of time this is a core  difference in design philosophy that I  think really demarcates dead cells as  novice Souls like in any significant way  if we look at other games that bear the  colorful moniker of souls like we'll see  games like the Serge and Nia which fall  much much more on the side of the souls  series slow and deliberate combat and  this isn't to say that you don't have to  be careful in dead cells combat or that  dead cells combat isn't high-stakes the  one hit can lead you to a pretty bad  situation but I think it's two of  different levels in Dark Souls because  the dead cells is supposed to be played  at a faster speed it simply doesn't play  like a Souls game it can be challenging  and my heart rate went through the roof  the first time I vanquished the bosses  but that's just a mark of a game with  satisfying combat not a Souls like or  derivative I really don't want to see  reviews and websites talking about how  this game shares attributes with Souls  sure that they will especially because  the developers do another aspect of the  gameplay that I want to talk about is  the enemy telegraphing system because of  the style of the game most of the  enemies have a very explicit Telegraph  right before they attack in the form of  an exclamation mark popping up above  them  I assume this is because most enemies  don't occupy much of the screen space so  assuming that the player would actually  be able to see the enemies is less  obvious movement Telegraph's would be  putting too much on the player  especially when there are multiple  enemies or a lot of particle effects  going on I understand why this would be  done as if players didn't have it they  could very well get mad that there isn't  a quote fair way to know when each enemy  is going to attack even though most  enemies do have an actual natural  Telegraph generally in the form of  winding up their attack in some way have  purely natural Telegraph's though I  don't know how plausible that would be  in a game like this it works well in the  souls games because the enemies in those  games are much larger relative to the  screen so they're much easier to see I  would think I'd be able to manage it in  game without the extra exclamation mark  is a lot of the National Telegraph's  are pretty decently shown so it's a  great but it's not the biggest one now  the replayability of the game due to its  roguelike run based design is a pretty  big plus to me it's the same kind of  thing as Binding of Isaac or enter the  gungeon with the games based around  repeatedly playing through the same  levels to make this not a boring slog  pick up some power-ups spawn differently  and each level is never the exact same  ever and from this procedural generation  is used to achieve this I think it's  used really well in this case you always  know generally what a level is like  after you've been through it but you're  never going to be running the exact same  corridors or memorizing maps to be fair  I haven't gotten to the late-game yet so  I can't say how it completely holds up  but it seems like dead cells does a  pretty good job of keeping high  replayability you need many many hours  of content on to some other aspects of  the game the aesthetics is high level  notch  visual design graphics sound design and  soundtracks are all done quite well the  atmospheres of each level are extremely  distinct and the art and music  immediately make that a reality  pixel art can sometimes be a hit or miss  with devs sometimes going really  simplistic with  number of pixels and amount of detail  dead cells doesn't have an immense  amount of detail but it does have enough  for it to not be a detriment the layered  design from foreground to double  distance background thanks traversing  back and forth in each level feel  dynamic and helps bring the world to  life the player design is unique  compared to the world so you stand out  enough to always be decently sure of  where you are though you don't take up  that much of the screen and with a  couple enemies launching attacks at you  the effects can get pretty intense there  have been a few times where I felt like  I lost where I was but it hasn't been a  major problem the enemy designs are  irrelevant coming across a new enemy  feels like a surprise because they're  generally unlike anything previously  seen the sounds are mostly good at doing  their job and the music always helps  build the atmosphere I don't have any  strong opinion either way about the  sounds they're decent and fulfilled what  they need to do but I do think the music  was created and chosen intelligently  because most of the levels are so  distinct they should have music that  helps illustrate those distinctions and  it does the seaside almost jaunty tunes  that accompany pirate zombies of the  stilt village are quite at odds with the  percussion heavy driving soundtrack of  the ramparts as they should be because  those are completely different thematic  zones this again helps the world feel  much more alive than it otherwise would  have without these features I should  probably discuss the story - it's there  but it's easy to miss most of the story  is learning about the lore of the island  that the game takes place on and the  different zones this is often done in  loading screens before each zone it's  definitely a more minor feature in my  opinion but it does give context to a  lot of the areas you explore and fight  in so it does help with the immersion  and world building of it all even if  it's to a small extent the player  character seems to have a good sense of  humor to him/her it that makes for some  slightly comical interactions which  helps lift the mood in an often intense  game overall the game has character you  can tell there was a clear design that  was faithfully executed with the result  being a unique and satisfying experience  I would give dead cells as a solid  recommendation especially to anyone who  has played or enjoyed Metroidvania or  roguelikes  it's pretty easy to jump into and it has  a lot in terms of content and room  master the combat satisfying gameplay  coupled with a nice aesthetic package  makes Ted sells a solid grab even if it  isn't a 2d Dark Souls

